re you can tell us about which  will mandate that vehicles must make  noise right well I think the difference  here is that it's a luxury it's a luxury  aspect when you're inside the car right  so you want to be in a car that is quiet  and soothing if you like a luxury ride  and you feel a little bit cocooned from  the hurly-burly of the city around you  but cars even luxury cars on the outside  make noise their their engines make  noise so the issue be can really came to  prominence when there was a conversion  from a traditional internal combustion  engine to essentially an electric  generator which is at low speeds very  very quiet and when we look at the  actual number of fatalities and  incidents of pedestrians being hit by  vehicles is there a significant increase  that we can see over recent years  yes Nyssa did some studies around this  and we're not talking about fatalities  thankfully because these tend to be  low-speed incidents they tend to be  things that happen when a vehicle is  backing out when a vehicle is making a  turn when a vehicle is in a parking lot  although they have trouble isolating  that in terms of the study but the fact  is that when there is an accident that  accidents involving electric vehicles or  hybrid electric vehicles are 35 percent  higher for pedestrians and 57 percent  higher for cyclist bicyclists both of  those are huge issues here in New York  City where we are and I'm curious  whether some of the new so-called safety  features and I say so-called  not because I don't believe that that's  what they are because usually they're  under this umbrella like backup cameras  proximity sensors blind spot indicators  do those counteract in any sense the the  danger posed by the quiet vehicles or  are we really talking about two totally  separate lines of thought here I haven't  seen any in any of the research that  Nitsa did up to this point a  consideration of whether those would  help common sense would tell you it's  got to be helpful because particularly  in the case of backing up that's a  particular issue I  think for both pedestrians and cyclists  and and the motorists because you do  have sort of inhibited vision I would  think that backup cameras any kind of  alert system some of the blind spot  warning systems also have rear collision  warnings have to be helpful to sort of  give the driver of a hybrid or Eevee a  heads up that they are sort of in  stealth mode if you will and that the  pedestrian or cyclist is not aware of  them so it's got to be helpful in my  experience driving and I try to drive as  little as possible within New York City  but I Drive pretty frequently to leave  and come back from the city I find that  so much of what I see going on on the  road that is wrong in other words people  who clearly are they're going 30 miles  under the speed limit you drive by and  they're on a cell phone they're wildly  changing lanes signaling in the opposite  direction than the direction they're  going I'm sure you know all of the stuff  that that is that is so prevalent so  often I feel like no matter how much  attention I'm paying as the so-called  good driver good guy with a car I can  really only limit the risk posed to me  by other drivers to a certain extent and  that to a certain extent there are just  so many distracted and otherwise bad  guys with a car so to speak that I can't  really shield myself or cocoon myself  completely apply that to this issue of  quiet cars and pedestrians and is there  as the driver can't what can you be  doing can you truly protect yourself  from a pedestrian who may be crossing  the street where there is no crosswalk  but also from the point of view of the  pedestrian can you be completely  inoculated from the possibility of a car  hitting you simply by following the  rules I think clearly the answer is no  clearly the answer is no I think for all  of us whether we're and and probably  like you I on any given day may be a  pedestrian or maybe 